from author import Bank,save_batch
banks=[]
b=Bank('subject-02','networks','circuit-analysis','Networks & Signals · Multi-Topic Reasoning','005');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'Two nodes v1,v2 have nodal equations 3v1−v2=5 and −v1+2v2=0, with currents in mA and conductances in mS. Find v1 in volts.',2,'Linear nodal system; consistent mS/mA units','Solve for v1.','Coupled nodal analysis',r'Gv=i',r'The second equation gives v2=v1/2. | Substitute into the first: (3−1/2)v1=5. | Thus 2.5v1=5 and v1=2 V; v2=1 V also satisfies both equations.','Use simultaneous node equations; grounding the second node changes the circuit.',difficulty='easy',unit='V')
b.n(r'Under the passive sign convention, a load has RMS phasors V=10∠0° V and I=2∠−60° A. Find its average absorbed power in watts.',10,'RMS V=10; RMS I=2; current lags voltage by 60°','Find Re(VI*).','Complex power',r'S=V_{rms}I_{rms}^*,\quad P=VI\cos\phi',r'The current is conjugated in complex power, giving phase +60°. | The apparent-power magnitude is 10×2=20 VA. | Its real part is 20cos60°=10 W.','RMS phasors do not require a further factor 1/2.',topic='ac-steady-state',unit='W')
b.n(r'A delta network has each side 9 Ω. Find each resistance in its equivalent balanced star in ohms.',3,'Balanced delta; three identical 9 Ω sides','Find one star arm.','Delta-to-star conversion',r'R_Y=R_\Delta^2/(3R_\Delta)',r'Each star arm is the product of its two adjoining delta sides divided by the sum of all three sides. | Here that is 9×9/(9+9+9). | The result is 81/27=3 Ω.','For a balanced network the delta resistance is three times a star arm.',topic='network-parameters',unit='Ω')
b.m(r'For the unilateral Laplace transform with initial value x(0−)=3, the transform of dx/dt is:','A',[r'$sX(s)-3$',r'$sX(s)+3$',r'$X(s)/s$',r'$sX(s)$ always'],'Unilateral transform starting at 0−; initial state 3','Include the derivative initial-condition term.','Unilateral derivative transform',r'\mathcal L_u\{x\prime\}=sX(s)-x(0^-)',r'Integration by parts moves the derivative to the exponential kernel. | The lower-limit boundary contributes −x(0−). | Substituting the given initial value yields sX(s)−3, option A.','A nonzero initial condition cannot be discarded.',topic='laplace-transform')
b.n(r'A causal continuous-time signal has Laplace transform X(s)=1/[(s+1)(s+3)]. Find x(0+).',0,'Strictly proper rational transform; causal ordinary exponential response','Find initial signal value.','Initial-value theorem',r'x(0^+)=\lim_{s\to\infty}sX(s)',r'There is no impulse term because X is strictly proper with denominator degree 2. | sX(s)=s/(s²+4s+3). | The ratio tends to zero, so x(0+)=0.','Apply the initial-value theorem only after checking that an impulse does not make the ordinary value undefined.',topic='laplace-transform')
b.n(r'A two-tap FIR filter is y[n]=x[n]−x[n−1]. Find |H(eʲΩ)| at Ω=π/2.',1.4142135623730951,'FIR taps 1,−1; Ω=π/2 rad/sample','Find frequency-response magnitude.','Discrete-time frequency response',r'H(e^{j\Omega})=1-e^{-j\Omega}',r'At Ω=π/2 the delayed exponential is −j. | Thus H=1+j. | Its magnitude is √(1²+1²)=√2≈1.414213562.','Do not subtract magnitudes before combining complex contributions.',topic='digital-filter-design')
b.n(r'The 4-point DFT uses X[k]=Σₙ₌₀³x[n]exp(−j2πkn/4). For x=[1,0,−1,0], find the real part of X[1].',2,'Four-point sequence [1,0,−1,0]; forward DFT convention','Find Re X[1].','DFT evaluation',r'X[k]=\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}x[n]e^{-j2\pi kn/N}',r'Only n=0 and n=2 contribute. | At k=1 their terms are 1 and (−1)e⁻ʲπ=1. | X[1]=2, purely real.','The negative time-domain sample multiplies a negative complex exponential at this bin.',topic='discrete-time-transforms')
b.s(r'For a passive reciprocal two-port described by finite z-parameters, which statements hold?',['A','C'],['z12 = z21','z11 = z22 is required by reciprocity alone','An open-circuit output measurement is used to determine z11 and z21 from port-1 excitation','The short-circuit input current always directly equals z11'],'Finite open-circuit impedance parameters; reciprocity','Identify z-parameter definitions and symmetry.','Two-port impedance parameters',r'\begin{pmatrix}V_1\\V_2\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}z_{11}&z_{12}\\z_{21}&z_{22}\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}I_1\\I_2\end{pmatrix}',r'With I2=0, the ratios V1/I1 and V2/I1 define z11 and z21. | Reciprocity imposes z12=z21 but does not imply geometrical symmetry z11=z22. | A z-parameter has resistance units, not current units. Select A and C.','Reciprocity and port symmetry are not identical conditions.',topic='network-parameters')
b.n(r'For x[n]=(1/3)ⁿu[n], find the radius of the inner boundary of the causal bilateral z-transform ROC.',0.3333333333333333,'Right-sided geometric sequence; bilateral z transform','Find the pole magnitude bounding the causal ROC.','Z-transform ROC',r'X(z)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}(1/(3z))^n',r'The geometric series converges when |1/(3z)|<1. | This is equivalent to |z|>1/3. | The inner-boundary radius is 1/3; the boundary itself is excluded.','A rational expression alone does not specify the ROC or causality.',topic='discrete-time-transforms')
b.n(r'An initially charged RC circuit has v(0)=1 V, final voltage 4 V, and τ=2 s. Find v(2 ln3) in volts.',3,'Initial voltage 1 V; final 4 V; positive first-order time constant 2 s','Find the response at the stated time.','General first-order state transition',r'v(t)=v_\infty+(v_0-v_\infty)e^{-t/\tau}',r'The initial deviation from the final state is 1−4=−3 V. | At t=2 ln3, the decay factor is 1/3. | The voltage is 4−3/3=3 V.','Natural and forced response must combine to satisfy the nonzero initial state.',topic='transient-analysis',unit='V')
b.s(r'For discrete-time causal FIR filters with a finite number of finite-valued taps, which statements hold?',['A','B','D'],['They are BIBO stable','Their impulse response is absolutely summable','Every such filter has a nonzero DC gain','Their output is a finite weighted sum of present/past input samples'],'Causal finite impulse response; finite coefficients','Select necessary FIR properties.','FIR support and summability',r'y[n]=\sum_{k=0}^{M}b_kx[n-k]',r'A finite sum of finite tap magnitudes is finite, satisfying the LTI BIBO condition. | The causal convolution uses only present and previous input samples. | DC gain is the tap sum, which can be zero (for example [1,−1]). Select A, B and D.','A stable filter can reject DC entirely.',topic='digital-filter-design')
b.n(r'A real sequence x=[1,2,3,4] has a 4-point DFT under the unnormalized forward convention. Find (1/4)Σₖ₌₀³|X[k]|².',30,'DFT forward unnormalized; sequence values 1,2,3,4','Find the normalized frequency-energy sum.','DFT Parseval identity',r'\sum_n|x[n]|^2=\frac1N\sum_k|X[k]|^2',r'The DFT orthogonality makes the stated normalized spectral energy equal to time-domain energy. | Sum 1²+2²+3²+4². | The result is 1+4+9+16=30.','Normalization depends on the DFT convention; the factor 1/4 is given.',topic='discrete-time-transforms',difficulty='hard')

b=Bank('subject-09','aptitude','quantitative-aptitude','General Aptitude · Quantitative & Logical Reasoning','005');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'A quantity increases by 20% and then decreases by 20%. Find the net percentage decrease from its original value.',4,'Successive multiplicative changes +20% and −20%','Find the net decrease percentage.','Successive percentages',r'\frac{Q_f}{Q_0}=(1+0.2)(1-0.2)',r'Take an initial quantity of 100 units. | It first becomes 120 and then 120×0.8=96. | The final value is 4 units below 100, a 4% decrease.','The two percentages use different bases and do not cancel.',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'A and B finish a job alone in 6 and 3 hours respectively. At constant independent work rates, find their completion time together in hours.',2,'Rates 1/6 and 1/3 job per hour','Find the reciprocal combined rate.','Work rates',r'1/T=1/T_A+1/T_B',r'Add the independent rates: 1/6+1/3=1/2 job/hour. | The whole job therefore takes 1/(1/2) hours. | Combined completion time is 2 hours.','Add work rates, not completion times.',unit='hours')
b.n(r'How many distinct arrangements of the letters in LEVEL are possible?',30,'Five letters; L repeated twice; E repeated twice; V once','Count distinct letter permutations.','Permutations with repetitions',r'N=\frac{5!}{2!2!}',r'If the five positions were all distinct there would be 5!=120 permutations. | Exchanging the identical L letters or identical E letters does not create a new arrangement. | Divide by 2!×2!=4 to get 30.','Do not divide by an additional 2 for V, which occurs only once.')
b.m(r'All members of set A belong to set B. No member of B belongs to C. Which conclusion necessarily follows?','C',['All C belong to A','Some A belong to C','No A belongs to C','Some B must belong to A'],'A⊆B; B∩C empty; sets may be empty','Choose the logically entailed conclusion.','Set-based deduction',r'A\subseteq B,\ B\cap C=\varnothing\Rightarrow A\cap C=\varnothing',r'Any hypothetical member of A is also a member of B. | Since no B member can be in C, that same member cannot be in C. | Thus A and C are disjoint. The premises do not assert that A is nonempty, so an existential conclusion is not forced.','Universal statements do not necessarily assert existence.',topic='analytical-aptitude')
b.n(r'Find the next term in the sequence 2, 6, 12, 20, 30 if its rule is aₙ=n(n+1) for n≥1.',42,'Rule explicitly specified as n(n+1)','Compute the sixth term.','Numerical relations',r'a_6=6(6+1)',r'The listed five terms correspond to n=1 through n=5. | The next index is 6. | a6=6×7=42; its increment from 30 is 12.','A finite list alone does not determine a unique rule; use the rule stated in the question.',topic='analytical-aptitude',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'A right circular cylinder has radius 2 cm and height 3 cm. Find its volume divided by π, in cm³.',12,'Radius 2 cm; height 3 cm','Find V/π.','Cylinder volume',r'V=\pi r^2h',r'The base area is π×2²=4π cm². | Multiply by height 3 cm to get 12π cm³. | The requested value V/π is 12 cm³.','The prompt asks for volume divided by π, not the full decimal volume.',unit='cm³')
b.n(r'In a class of 40 students, 25 study subject A, 20 study B and 10 study both. How many study neither?',5,'Population 40; |A|=25; |B|=20; |A∩B|=10','Find the complement of A∪B.','Inclusion–exclusion',r'|A\cup B|=|A|+|B|-|A\cap B|',r'Adding 25+20 counts the 10 students in both subjects twice. | Subtract their overlap once: union size 35. | The number studying neither is 40−35=5.','Do not subtract the overlap twice when forming the union.')
b.s(r'Which statements about the positive numbers a=2, b=8 are true?',['A','C','D'],['Their arithmetic mean is 5','Their geometric mean is 5','Their geometric mean is 4','Their harmonic mean is 3.2'],'Two positive observations 2 and 8','Calculate and compare the three means.','Arithmetic, geometric and harmonic means',r'AM=\frac{a+b}{2},\quad GM=\sqrt{ab},\quad HM=\frac2{1/a+1/b}',r'AM=(2+8)/2=5 and GM=√16=4. | HM=2/(1/2+1/8)=2/(5/8)=16/5=3.2. | Select A, C and D; the values also satisfy AM≥GM≥HM.','These different means answer different aggregation questions.')
b.n(r'Two fair six-sided dice are rolled independently. Find the probability that their sum equals 7.',0.16666666666666666,'36 equally likely ordered pairs of die outcomes','Find P(sum=7).','Counting ordered outcomes',r'P(E)=N_E/N_S',r'The pairs are (1,6),(2,5),(3,4),(4,3),(5,2),(6,1). | There are 6 favorable ordered pairs out of 36. | Probability is 6/36=1/6.','The two dice form ordered outcomes, even when the sum ignores order.')
b.m(r'Choose the grammatically correct sentence for the stated singular subject “Each of the instruments”.','B',['Each of the instruments are calibrated.','Each of the instruments is calibrated.','Each of the instruments were calibrated tomorrow.','Each of the instruments have calibrated.'],'Subject headed by singular distributive pronoun Each','Select correct subject–verb agreement.','Subject–verb agreement',r'\text{Each}\ \longrightarrow\ \text{singular finite verb}',r'The grammatical head of the subject is Each, not instruments inside the of-phrase. | A singular present-tense subject takes is in this passive construction. | Therefore “Each of the instruments is calibrated” is correct. The other options have agreement or tense/structure errors.','Do not make the verb agree with a nearby plural noun inside a prepositional phrase.',topic='verbal-aptitude')
b.n(r'Point P=(2,1) is rotated by 90° counterclockwise about the origin, then translated by (3,−1). Find the final x-coordinate.',2,'Rotation first; translation second; Cartesian coordinates','Find the x-coordinate after both transformations.','Planar rotation and translation',r'(x,y)\xrightarrow{90^\circ\ CCW}(-y,x)',r'Rotating (2,1) gives (−1,2). | Add the translation vector (3,−1) to obtain (2,1). | The final x-coordinate is 2. Rotation and translation generally do not commute.','Apply transformations in the specified order.',topic='spatial-aptitude',difficulty='medium')
b.s(r'Three jobs A,B,C must be ordered. A must precede B, and C must not be first. Which complete orders satisfy both constraints?',['A','C'],['A B C','C A B','A C B','B A C'],'Three distinct jobs; two precedence/position constraints','Select every feasible full ordering.','Constraint-based ordering',r'\operatorname{pos}(A)<\operatorname{pos}(B),\quad\operatorname{pos}(C)\ne1',r'A B C places A before B and C third, so it is valid. | C A B violates the first-position rule, whereas A C B satisfies both rules. | B A C violates A-before-B. Select A and C.','Check every constraint independently for each proposed order.',topic='analytical-aptitude',difficulty='medium')

b=Bank('mixed-01','mathematics','linear-algebra','Mixed Revision · Systems & Signals','005');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'The matrix A=[[0,−2],[1,3]] has two real eigenvalues. Find the larger eigenvalue.',2,'Real 2 × 2 matrix; trace 3; determinant 2','Find the larger characteristic root.','Matrix characteristic polynomial',r'\lambda^2-\operatorname{tr}(A)\lambda+\det A=0',r'The trace is 3 and determinant is 2. | The characteristic polynomial is λ²−3λ+2=(λ−1)(λ−2). | The eigenvalues are 1 and 2, so the larger is 2.','Eigenvalues are not generally equal to diagonal entries for a nondiagonal matrix.')
b.n(r'A DC node connects to a 6 V source through 2 kΩ and to ground through 1 kΩ. With no other branches, find node voltage in volts.',2,'Source 6 V; source-side resistor 2 kΩ; ground-side resistor 1 kΩ','Find the unloaded divider voltage.','Nodal current balance',r'(v-6)/2+v/1=0',r'Using kΩ and mA, KCL is (v−6)/2+v=0. | Multiply by 2: 3v−6=0. | Thus v=2 V.','The voltage is measured across the ground-side resistor.',subject='networks',topic='circuit-analysis',unit='V',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'An ideal binary 5-bit counter cycles through all states and returns to its starting state. How many clock edges occur in one complete cycle?',32,'Five flip-flops; full binary state cycle','Find the modulus of the counter.','Binary state capacity',r'M=2^n',r'Each bit has two possible values, so five bits represent 2⁵ distinct states. | A binary counter visits one state per clock edge and repeats after all states. | Its cycle therefore spans 32 active clock edges.','Five bits do not imply only five states.',subject='digital',topic='sequential-circuits',difficulty='easy')
b.m(r'For an ideal NMOS with VGS>VTH, which condition defines the long-channel triode region?','C',['VDS≥VGS−VTH','VGS<VTH','0≤VDS<VGS−VTH','VDS must be zero only'],'NMOS; positive drain voltage; long-channel model','Choose the channel-present unsaturated region.','MOS region conditions',r'0\le V_{DS}<V_{GS}-V_{TH}',r'Positive gate overdrive forms an inversion channel. | When drain voltage remains below the overdrive, that channel has not pinched off at the drain. | This is the triode region, option C; larger VDS leads to the ideal saturation condition.','Gate overdrive must first be positive; the drain inequality alone is insufficient.',subject='devices',topic='mosfet')
b.n(r'An ideal noninverting amplifier has 9 kΩ from output to its inverting input and 1 kΩ from that input to ground. Find its closed-loop voltage gain.',10,'Ideal op-amp; stable negative feedback; Rf=9 kΩ; Rg=1 kΩ','Find vo/vi.','Noninverting feedback divider',r'A_v=1+R_f/R_g',r'The feedback node is vo×1/(9+1)=vo/10. | Ideal linear feedback sets it equal to the noninverting input vi. | Thus vo=10vi and gain is 10.','The noninverting gain includes the unity term.',subject='analog',topic='operational-amplifiers')
b.n(r'A stable system has H(s)=2/(s+2). For a unit impulse input, find output at t=ln2.',0.5,'Causal transfer; impulse input; zero initial state','Find the impulse response at ln2.','Inverse Laplace transform',r'h(t)=2e^{-2t}u(t)',r'The transform pair 1/(s+a) ↔ e⁻ᵃᵗu(t) gives h=2e⁻²ᵗu(t). | At t=ln2, the exponential is 1/4. | The output is 2×1/4=0.5.','A unit impulse produces the impulse response, not the unit-step response.',subject='control',topic='time-response')
b.n(r'An equiprobable 8-symbol source emits 2000 symbols/s. Find its entropy rate in kbit/s.',6,'Eight equally likely choices per independent source symbol; 2000 symbols/s','Find source information rate.','Entropy of an equiprobable alphabet',r'H=\log_2M,\quad R_H=R_sH',r'Each source symbol has log₂8=3 bits of entropy. | At 2000 symbols/s this is 6000 bits/s. | The entropy rate is 6 kbit/s.','Entropy rate assumes the stated independence; correlations could lower it.',subject='communications',topic='information-theory',unit='kbit/s')
b.n(r'A lossless line has a purely resistive load matched to Z0=75 Ω. Find its voltage reflection coefficient.',0,'ZL=Z0=75 Ω','Find Γ at a matched termination.','Impedance matching',r'\Gamma=(Z_L-Z_0)/(Z_L+Z_0)',r'The load equals the characteristic impedance. | The numerator of Γ is 75−75=0 while its denominator is nonzero. | Therefore Γ=0 and no reflected traveling voltage wave is produced.', 'A matched load need not be a short circuit.',subject='electromagnetics',topic='transmission-lines',difficulty='easy')
b.s(r'Which Boolean expressions are identically equal to A XOR B?',['A','D'],['(A AND NOT B) OR (NOT A AND B)','A OR B','A AND B','(A OR B) AND NOT(A AND B)'],'Two Boolean inputs','Select every equivalent XOR form.','Boolean equivalence',r'A\oplus B=A\bar B+\bar A B',r'XOR is 1 exactly when the inputs differ. | A explicitly enumerates the two unequal input cases. | D admits at least one high input but excludes both high. OR and AND alone do not match XOR. Select A and D.','Test the input pair (1,1), which distinguishes OR from XOR.',subject='digital',topic='boolean-algebra')
b.s(r'Which causal continuous-time transfer functions are BIBO stable without cancellations?',['A','C'],[r'$1/(s+1)$',r'$1/(s-1)$',r'$1/(s^2+2s+2)$',r'$1/s$'],'Causal rational systems; no hidden cancellations','Select stable pole sets.','Left-half-plane stability',r'\operatorname{Re}p_i<0',r'A has pole −1. C has poles −1±j, both with negative real part. | B has pole +1 and D has a pole at zero. | Strict BIBO stability holds only for A and C.','A pole on the stability boundary does not satisfy strict BIBO stability.',subject='control',topic='stability',difficulty='hard')
b.n(r'A card is drawn uniformly from integers 1 through 10. Given that it is at least 6, find the probability it is even.',0.6,'Conditioned sample space {6,7,8,9,10}','Find P(even | at least 6).','Conditional counting',r'P(A|B)=|A\cap B|/|B|',r'Five equally likely values remain after conditioning. | Even values among them are 6,8,10, giving three favorable cases. | The probability is 3/5=0.6.','Use the reduced sample space after conditioning.',subject='aptitude',topic='quantitative-aptitude')
b.n(r'A 12 V Thevenin source with 1 Ω internal resistance supplies a positive load R. The load dissipates 16 W. Find either possible value of R in ohms; both positive solutions are accepted.',0.14589803375031546,'Vth=12 V; internal resistance 1 Ω; load power 16 W; R>0','Find either positive load satisfying the power constraint.','Inverse load-power problem',r'P_L=144R/(1+R)^2',r'Set 144R/(1+R)²=16 and simplify to R²−7R+1=0. | The quadratic roots are (7−3√5)/2≈0.145898034 Ω and (7+3√5)/2≈6.854101966 Ω. | Both are positive and give the specified power. Any value between the roots does not generally solve the equation.','The inverse power problem has two admissible positive loads; do not discard one without a domain condition.',difficulty='hard',subject='networks',topic='network-theorems',unit='Ω')
q=b.questions[-1];q.pop('acceptedRange');q['acceptedRanges']=[[0.14589793375031546,0.14589813375031546],[6.854101280839488,6.854102651659881]];q['solution']['finalAnswer']='0.145898034 Ω OR 6.854101966 Ω'

b=Bank('mixed-02','mathematics','calculus','Mixed Revision · Models & Measurements','005');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'For f(x)=ln x on x>0, find f′(2).',0.5,'Natural logarithm; positive-domain argument 2','Find the local derivative.','Logarithmic derivative',r'\frac{d}{dx}\ln x=1/x',r'The logarithm is differentiable on the positive real domain. | Its derivative is the reciprocal of the argument. | At x=2, f′=1/2=0.5.','The derivative of ln x is not ln(1/x).',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'A 3 Ω resistor and a reactance +j4 Ω are in series at one sinusoidal frequency. Find the impedance magnitude in ohms.',5,'Series impedance 3+j4 Ω','Find |Z|.','Complex impedance magnitude',r'|Z|=\sqrt{R^2+X^2}',r'Series impedances add as complex numbers, giving 3+j4. | Its magnitude is the Euclidean norm of the real and imaginary parts. | √(9+16)=5 Ω.','Add R and X as orthogonal components, not scalar magnitudes.',subject='networks',topic='ac-steady-state',unit='Ω')
b.n(r'An unsigned 6-bit word is 101101₂. Find its decimal value.',45,'Binary digits 1,0,1,1,0,1 with place values 32,16,8,4,2,1','Convert the unsigned word.','Binary weighted representation',r'N=\sum_i b_i2^i',r'The high positions contribute 32,8,4 and 1. | The other two positions contribute zero. | The decimal value is 32+8+4+1=45.','The word is explicitly unsigned; do not treat its leading bit as a sign.',subject='digital',topic='number-representations',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'An ideal BJT has β=99 and emitter current 1 mA in forward active operation. Find base current in μA.',10,'β=IC/IB=99; IE=1 mA','Find base current.','BJT current partition',r'I_E=(\beta+1)I_B',r'Emitter current is collector plus base current. | With IC=99IB, emitter current is 100IB. | IB=1 mA/100=0.01 mA=10 μA.','Do not divide emitter current by β alone.',subject='devices',topic='bjt',unit='μA')
b.m(r'A constant input is applied to an ideal inverting differentiator after all switching impulses have passed. Its output is:','C',['A continuously rising ramp','A nonzero constant proportional to elapsed time','Zero','Always at a supply rail'],'Ideal differentiation; steady constant input; linear operation','Find the steady derivative response.','Op-amp differentiation',r'V_o=-RC\,dV_i/dt',r'A constant input has zero time derivative. | Multiplication by −RC therefore gives zero output after any initial transition. | The steady output is zero, option C. This statement excludes the switching impulse explicitly.','A differentiator responds to changes, unlike an integrator.',subject='analog',topic='operational-amplifiers')
b.n(r'For characteristic polynomial s²+4s+8, find its damping ratio ζ.',0.7071067811865475,'Standard second-order polynomial with ωn²=8','Find ζ.','Second-order coefficient matching',r'2\zeta\omega_n=4,\quad\omega_n=\sqrt8',r'Matching the constant coefficient gives ωn=√8. | The coefficient of s requires ζ=4/(2√8). | Hence ζ=1/√2≈0.7071067812.','Do not set ωn to the constant coefficient before taking its square root.',subject='control',topic='time-response')
b.n(r'A uniform 4-bit quantizer spans 0 to 2 V in equal-width bins. Find the bin width in millivolts using span/2ⁿ.',125,'Span 2 V; 16 equal bins; specified bin convention','Find quantization step.','Uniform quantization',r'\Delta=V_{span}/2^n',r'Four bits define 16 bins. | Each bin has width 2/16=0.125 V. | Convert to millivolts: 0.125×1000=125 mV.','Do not use 2ⁿ−1 when the prompt specifies equal-bin span/2ⁿ.',subject='digital',topic='data-converters',unit='mV')
b.n(r'A 2 kHz sine message uses conventional double-sideband AM around a 100 kHz carrier. Find the separation between its lower and upper sideband frequencies in kHz.',4,'Single 2 kHz message tone; carrier 100 kHz','Find fUSB−fLSB.','AM spectral sidebands',r'f_{USB}=f_c+f_m,\quad f_{LSB}=f_c-f_m',r'The sidebands lie at 102 kHz and 98 kHz. | Their separation is 102−98. | The requested bandwidth between those components is 4 kHz.','The sideband separation is twice the message frequency.',subject='communications',topic='analog-communication',unit='kHz')
b.n(r'A wave propagates at 2×10⁸ m/s with frequency 100 MHz. Find its wavelength in meters.',2,'Phase speed 2×10⁸ m/s; frequency 10⁸ Hz','Find λ.','Wave velocity relation',r'\lambda=v_p/f',r'Each period advances the phase pattern by one wavelength. | Divide 2×10⁸ m/s by 10⁸ cycles/s. | The wavelength is 2 m.','Use the propagation speed in the given medium rather than automatically using c.',subject='electromagnetics',topic='wave-propagation',unit='m')
b.s(r'Which statements about a lossless, open-ended transmission line at its load plane hold?',['A','C'],['The load voltage reflection coefficient is +1','The load current reflection coefficient is +1 using the same +z current reference for both waves','The total current at the open termination is zero','The load voltage must be zero'],'Ideal open termination; forward and backward current waves referenced to +z','Select open-circuit standing-wave facts.','Traveling-wave termination conditions',r'\Gamma_V=+1,\quad\Gamma_I=-1',r'An open end enforces zero net current. | The reflected current cancels the incident current, whereas reflected voltage adds to incident voltage. | Thus ΓV=+1 and current reflection is −1 with the stated common reference. Select A and C.','Voltage and current reflection coefficients have opposite signs in a common propagation reference.',subject='electromagnetics',topic='transmission-lines',difficulty='hard')
b.s(r'For events A and B with P(A)=0.4, P(B)=0.5 and independence, which statements hold?',['A','B','D'],['P(A∩B)=0.2','P(A∪B)=0.7','A and B are mutually exclusive','P(A|B)=0.4'],'Independent events with nonzero probabilities','Select the correct event probabilities.','Independence and inclusion–exclusion',r'P(A\cap B)=P(A)P(B)',r'Independence gives intersection 0.4×0.5=0.2, so the events are not mutually exclusive. | The union probability is 0.4+0.5−0.2=0.7. | Conditional probability is intersection divided by 0.5, which returns 0.4. Select A, B and D.','Mutual exclusivity and independence are different for events of positive probability.',subject='aptitude',topic='quantitative-aptitude')
b.n(r'A stable causal first-order system has H(s)=K/(s+a), with K,a positive. Its unit-step final value is 2 and |H(j3)|=1.6. Find K.',8,'Positive a,K; step DC gain 2; magnitude 1.6 at 3 rad/s','Determine numerator gain from both response constraints.','Identification from DC and frequency response',r'K/a=2,\quad K/\sqrt{a^2+9}=1.6',r'The final step value gives K=2a. | Substitute into the frequency constraint: 4a²=2.56(a²+9), so 1.44a²=23.04 and a²=16. | Stability/positivity selects a=4, hence K=8.','The DC gain supplies an additional equation; the frequency magnitude alone cannot determine both unknowns.',subject='networks',topic='lti-systems',difficulty='hard')

b=Bank('mixed-03','mathematics','probability-and-statistics','Mixed Revision · Design Checks & Invariants','005');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'If X and Y are independent, Var(X)=2 and Var(Y)=3, find Var(2X−Y).',11,'Independent variables; variances 2 and 3','Find the variance of a weighted difference.','Variance scaling and addition',r'\operatorname{Var}(aX+bY)=a^2\operatorname{Var}(X)+b^2\operatorname{Var}(Y)',r'Independence makes covariance zero. | The weights are squared, so (2)²×2+(−1)²×3. | The variance is 8+3=11.','A minus sign on a variable does not produce a negative variance.')
b.n(r'Two equal 2 kΩ resistors form a DC divider from 8 V to ground. An additional 2 kΩ load is connected from midpoint to ground. Find the loaded midpoint voltage in volts.',2.6666666666666665,'Top 2 kΩ; bottom 2 kΩ; load 2 kΩ in parallel with bottom','Find the loaded divider voltage.','Loading of a voltage divider',r'V_o=V_s\frac{R_{bottom}\parallel R_L}{R_{top}+R_{bottom}\parallel R_L}',r'The lower resistor and load combine to 1 kΩ. | The source sees 2 kΩ in series with 1 kΩ, total 3 kΩ. | The loaded output is 8×1/3=8/3 V, below the unloaded 4 V.','A finite load changes the divider’s effective lower resistance.',subject='networks',topic='circuit-analysis',unit='V')
b.n(r'A positive-edge D flip-flop has setup time 2 ns and hold time 1 ns around a clock edge at t=10 ns. D is stable from t=7 ns to t=12 ns. Does it satisfy both constraints? Enter 1 for yes, 0 for no.',1,'Clock at 10 ns; required setup 2 ns and hold 1 ns; data stable over [7,12] ns','Assess the specified stability window.','Flip-flop timing aperture',r'[t_c-t_{setup},t_c+t_{hold}]=[8,11]\ \text{ns}',r'The required valid-data interval is from 8 to 11 ns. | The supplied stable interval [7,12] contains it completely. | Both constraints are met, so enter 1. This only assesses the stated deterministic timing bounds.','Check the post-edge hold interval as well as the pre-edge setup interval.',subject='digital',topic='sequential-circuits')
b.m(r'For an ideal PN diode at a fixed temperature and ideality factor 1, increasing forward bias by VT ln2 changes I+IS by a factor:','D',['1/2','1','ln2','2'],'Exact Shockley law; fixed IS and VT','Find the exponential-current scaling.','Diode exponential ratio',r'I+I_S=I_Se^{V/V_T}',r'Writing I+IS removes the −1 term exactly. | A bias increase VTln2 multiplies the exponential by exp(ln2). | The scaling factor is 2, option D.','The statement is exact for I+IS; current I itself only approximately doubles at large forward bias.',subject='devices',topic='pn-junctions')
b.n(r'An unloaded ideal voltage amplifier has gain −20 and a 10 mV input signal amplitude. Find the output signal amplitude magnitude in volts.',0.2,'Gain −20; input amplitude 0.010 V; linear unsaturated operation','Find output magnitude.','Small-signal gain',r'|v_o|=|A_v||v_i|',r'The negative gain indicates a phase inversion, not a negative amplitude magnitude. | Multiply 20 by 0.010 V. | The output amplitude magnitude is 0.2 V.','Separate signal magnitude from phase inversion.',subject='analog',topic='amplifiers',unit='V',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'For a closed-loop system with poles −2±j3, find the undamped natural frequency ωn in rad/s.',3.605551275463989,'Complex pole pair with real part −2 and imaginary parts ±3','Find pole radius.','Second-order pole geometry',r'\omega_n=\sqrt{\sigma^2+\omega_d^2}',r'The standard pole form is −ζωn±jωn√(1−ζ²). | Its magnitude is ωn. | √(2²+3²)=√13≈3.605551275 rad/s.','The imaginary part alone is the damped oscillation frequency.',subject='control',topic='time-response',unit='rad/s')
b.n(r'A memoryless channel uses four equiprobable, perfectly distinguishable symbols. Find its capacity in bits per channel use.',2,'Noiseless four-symbol alphabet','Find maximum information per use.','Noiseless discrete-channel capacity',r'C=\log_2M',r'A noiseless symbol identifies one of four possibilities without ambiguity. | Equal probabilities maximize entropy. | Capacity is log₂4=2 bits/use.','Capacity here is per channel use, not a rate per second.',subject='communications',topic='information-theory',unit='bits/use',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'A short-circuited lossless line is exactly one quarter wavelength long. In the ideal limit, what is the magnitude of its input admittance in siemens?',0,'ZL=0; line length λ/4; lossless ideal limit','Find |Yin| at the quarter-wave transformed short.','Quarter-wave short-to-open conversion',r'Z_{in}=jZ_0\tan(\beta l)',r'At l=λ/4, βl=π/2 and the input impedance tends to an open circuit. | The corresponding input admittance tends to zero. | Thus its magnitude is 0 S in the stated ideal limit.','A quarter-wave line transforms a short to an open, rather than preserving the termination.',subject='electromagnetics',topic='transmission-lines',unit='S')
b.s(r'Which are valid implications for a real square matrix A with det(A)≠0?',['A','B','D'],['A has a unique inverse','Ax=b has a unique solution for every b','A must be symmetric','The null space of A contains only the zero vector'],'Nonsingular square matrix','Select all consequences of invertibility.','Invertible matrix theorem',r'\det A\ne0\iff\operatorname{rank}A=n',r'Full rank gives an inverse and trivial homogeneous null space. | Every right-hand side then has the solution x=A⁻¹b, uniquely. | Symmetry is unrelated; an invertible triangular nonsymmetric matrix is a counterexample. Select A, B and D.','Nonsingularity does not imply orthogonality or symmetry.',subject='mathematics',topic='linear-algebra',difficulty='hard')
b.s(r'For ideal logic, which statements about a full adder are true?',['A','C','D'],['It adds three one-bit inputs A,B,Cin','Its carry output is A XOR B XOR Cin','Its sum output is A XOR B XOR Cin','Its carry is 1 when at least two input bits are 1'],'One-bit full adder with sum and carry outputs','Select valid output descriptions.','Full-adder logic',r'S=A\oplus B\oplus C_{in},\quad C_{out}=AB+AC_{in}+BC_{in}',r'The arithmetic total ranges from 0 to 3 and equals S+2Cout. | The sum is the parity bit; carry marks totals 2 or 3. | Thus A,C,D hold, whereas assigning XOR to carry is wrong.','A full adder differs from a half adder by its carry-in input.',subject='digital',topic='combinational-circuits')
b.n(r'A tank initially contains 20 L of a 30% solution. How many liters of pure water must be added to reduce the concentration to 20%, assuming additive volumes?',10,'Initial solute 30% of 20 L; added liquid has no solute','Find added water volume.','Mixture conservation',r'0.3(20)=0.2(20+x)',r'The solute amount remains 6 L-equivalent after dilution. | The new total volume must be 6/0.2=30 L. | Add 30−20=10 L of water.','The percentage denominator changes while the solute amount is conserved.',subject='aptitude',topic='quantitative-aptitude',unit='L')
b.n(r'Two matched amplifier stages use the same reference noise temperature. Stage 1 has power gain 10 dB and noise figure 3 dB; stage 2 has noise figure 10 dB. Find the total noise factor as a linear ratio.',2.8952623149688795,'Matched cascade; G1=10 dB; NF1=3 dB; NF2=10 dB; common reference temperature','Find the linear cascade noise factor.','Friis cascade noise',r'F_{tot}=F_1+(F_2-1)/G_1',r'Convert power quantities to linear ratios: G1=10, F1=10^0.3≈1.995262315 and F2=10. | Refer the second stage’s added noise to the input by dividing F2−1=9 by the first-stage gain 10. | Ftotal≈1.995262315+0.9=2.895262315.','Friis arithmetic uses linear power gain and linear noise factors, not their decibel values.',subject='communications',topic='noise',difficulty='hard')

